(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-vignettes"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 33-year-old man has recurrent tapioca-like vesicles on his palms confirmed clinically as dyshidrotic eczema. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["A topical antifungal","Oral prednisone tapered over three weeks","A high-potency topical corticosteroid","A low-potency topical corticosteroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High potency is specified first line here, with systemic corticosteroids reserved for severe cases. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman has adult-onset eczema that has not responded to appropriate topical therapy over several months. Her rash involves the eyelids and hands. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic test?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation","Skin-prick testing","Tzanck smear","Patch testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — patch testing is indicated for atypical, adult-onset or treatment-resistant disease, and eyelid and hand involvement raises contact allergy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has progressive recession of the frontotemporal hairline in a triangular pattern with thinning at the crown, developing over several years. His father and grandfather have the same pattern. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Alopecia areata","Alopecia totalis","Trichotillomania from chronic hair pulling","Androgenetic alopecia, male-pattern hair loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — androgen-dependent, with dihydrotestosterone chiefly responsible and a strong paternal influence on risk. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 146"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman has diffuse thinning over the central and parietal scalp with her frontal hairline preserved. Which is the most appropriate treatment consideration?","choices":["Oral finasteride prescribed as first-line therapy for female-pattern hair loss at any age after puberty","Intralesional corticosteroids","Antifungal shampoo","Oral antiandrogens such as combined oral contraceptives may be considered, since treatment options are limited"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — female-pattern hair loss has fewer options, and finasteride is not indicated in women. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 41-year-old intensive care nurse reports several weeks of worsening dryness, redness and scaling of both hands, worst over the dorsal surfaces. She washes her hands more than thirty times a shift. The rash is well demarcated with a glazed appearance and there are no vesicles. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic contact dermatitis","Dyshidrotic eczema","Psoriasis","Irritant contact dermatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the commonest form of contact dermatitis, frequently occupational, with soaps and detergents as classic irritants and a glazed well-demarcated appearance. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman notices two smooth, round 3 cm bald patches on her scalp that appeared over a few weeks. The scalp is smooth without scale or erythema, and short hairs tapering toward the root are visible at the margins. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Alopecia areata","Tinea capitis","Trichotillomania","Androgenetic alopecia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exclamation point hairs (thinner at the root, normal calibre at the top) are pathognomonic, and the scalp is smooth without scale. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"A 30-year-old has extensive urushiol contact dermatitis covering both arms, the neck and part of the trunk. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral antihistamines alone","High-dose oral corticosteroids tapered over two to three weeks","High-potency topical corticosteroids alone","A five-day course of oral prednisone without any taper"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — extensive disease needs systemic therapy, and early discontinuation causes high rates of rebound. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man reports generalised itching and rough, tight skin each winter, worst on the shins. Examination shows fine scale with a few shallow fissures and scratch marks, without erythematous plaques. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Take short lukewarm showers with a gentle fragrance-free cleanser only where needed, and apply a thick ointment or cream within minutes of bathing","Take long hot showers each morning to hydrate the skin thoroughly, then apply a light lotion once the skin has completely dried","Use an antibacterial soap over the whole body twice daily","Apply a keratolytic containing urea to the fissured areas first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hot water and detergents are among the causes, and prompt moisturising after bathing is the key manoeuvre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 153"},{"q":"A 10-month-old has a diaper rash involving the inguinal folds with satellite papules beyond the main area. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic test?","choices":["Bacterial culture and sensitivity","Patch testing","Skin biopsy","Potassium hydroxide preparation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — fold involvement and satellite lesions suggest Candida, and identification of budding yeast or pseudohyphae supports it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has extensive perioral dermatitis that has persisted despite stopping her topical corticosteroid and simplifying her routine. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Topical high-potency corticosteroid","Oral tetracycline or doxycycline","Topical metronidazole","Oral prednisone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — more extensive or persistent disease may require an oral tetracycline. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with cutaneous lichen planus on the wrists has not improved with emollients. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Topical tacrolimus ointment","Oral prednisone","PUVA phototherapy","Superpotent topical steroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Topical tacrolimus is used for oral and vaginal disease, oral steroids for severe cases, and phototherapy for refractory disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 178"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has suspected lichen planus. Which biopsy finding confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Neutrophils aligned in a row at the dermal-epidermal junction","Munro microabscesses within the stratum corneum","Band-like infiltration of lymphocytes in the dermis","Acantholysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with hyperkeratosis without parakeratosis, basal vacuolisation, Civatte bodies and a saw-tooth rete ridge. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 177"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has a two-month history of intense itching followed by blisters on the trunk, axillae and inner thighs. Examination shows numerous tense bullae 1 to 3 cm across on urticarial bases; rubbing adjacent normal skin does not cause it to shear away. There is no oral involvement. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dyshidrotic eczema","Allergic contact dermatitis","Bullous pemphigoid","Pemphigus vulgaris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tense bullae, a negative Nikolsky sign, an itchy prodrome and onset after 60 are the picture. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man with pityriasis rosea asks what to expect. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It is self-limited and typically fades over 4 to 6 weeks, though darker skin may show post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation for several months","It requires a six-week course of oral antibiotics","It will persist indefinitely without phototherapy","It is highly contagious by direct skin contact, so he should avoid close contact with others until every last lesion has faded"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reassurance is the mainstay, with antihistamines or topical steroids used cautiously for itch. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has an oval scaly plaque on his chest that appeared ten days ago, and now smaller oval lesions are spreading down his trunk. Which is the most appropriate initial diagnostic approach?","choices":["Serum rapid plasma reagin","Clinical diagnosis based on the herald patch and the typical pattern","Skin biopsy of the herald patch","Potassium hydroxide preparation taken from the herald patch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the herald patch and cleavage-line distribution are enough. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has painful oral erosions and flaccid skin bullae. A biopsy has been taken. Which finding on that biopsy would establish the diagnosis?","choices":["Hyperkeratosis without parakeratosis and Civatte bodies","Acantholysis","Neutrophils aligned in a straight narrow row at the dermal-epidermal junction","Band-like lymphocytic infiltration of the dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — loss of keratinocyte-to-keratinocyte adhesion is the defining histological feature, with immunofluorescence and serum ELISA confirmatory. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 132"},{"q":"A 3-month-old infant has thick, greasy yellow scale adherent to the scalp, with mild erythema and no distress. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis","Tinea capitis","Psoriasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the scalp form is dandruff in adults and this presentation in infants, driven by Malassezia in sebum-rich areas. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman has erosive oral and vulvar lichen planus. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Hepatitis C is the established cause of this condition, so treating the infection will reliably clear the rash","Genital and erosive oral disease carry an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma, so ongoing surveillance is needed","Mucosal involvement resolves spontaneously and needs no follow-up","The condition is contagious to sexual partners"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is the reason mucosal involvement must be identified and followed. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman develops an itchy eruption under a new necklace and beneath the button of her jeans. Which allergen is most likely?","choices":["Bacitracin","Nickel","Urushiol","Neomycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — nickel is the most common metal allergen, and eruptions reproduce the shape of the item worn. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man develops an itchy violaceous papular eruption on his wrists two months after starting a new antihypertensive. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Review the medication list, since several drugs produce lichenoid reactions","Begin superpotent topical steroids without further history","Test for hepatitis C and treat it to clear the rash","Reassure that the eruption is self-limited and requires no action"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, sulfonamides, tetracyclines, hydrochlorothiazide, quinidine and some beta blockers are named. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 173"},{"q":"A 44-year-old with psoriasis has painful, stiff fingers each morning for over an hour, easing with activity. Examination shows a swollen sausage-shaped third digit, and the nails show pitting and separation of the plate from the bed. Which radiographic finding would you expect?","choices":["Subchondral sclerosis with marginal osteophytes","Periarticular osteopenia with symmetric erosions","Pencil-in-a-cup deformity","Bamboo spine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — psoriatic arthritis, associated with HLA-B27, affecting the distal and proximal interphalangeal joints while sparing the metacarpophalangeals. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 161"},{"q":"A 44-year-old janitor has chronic dry, fissured, glazed dermatitis of both hands from repeated detergent exposure. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Refer for patch testing before any treatment","Avoid the exposure and repair the barrier with emollients, sleeping in cotton gloves after applying a heavy emollient such as petroleum jelly","Begin a high-potency topical corticosteroid indefinitely, continuing it daily even once the hands have healed, without addressing the exposure","Begin oral doxycycline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with antihistamines such as hydroxyzine or diphenhydramine for itch. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has several intensely itchy round plaques on his lower legs, 2 to 4 cm across, light pink with scale and crusting. The plaques are uniform throughout without any central clearing. Which is the most appropriate initial diagnostic test?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation","Bacterial culture","Allergy patch testing","Punch biopsy for direct immunofluorescence"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tinea corporis is the main differential and is excluded by potassium hydroxide, though the absent central clearing already argues against it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 29-year-old hairdresser has a three-week history of intensely itchy small blisters along the sides of her fingers and on her palms. Examination shows grouped 1 to 2 mm vesicles described as resembling tapioca, on a poorly defined erythematous base, with surrounding dryness. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dyshidrotic eczema","Allergic contact dermatitis","Bullous pemphigoid","Nummular eczema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tapioca vesicles on the palms and lateral fingers are the defining picture; vesicles may coalesce into bullae before desquamating. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with widespread flaccid bullae and painful oral erosions has a biopsy showing acantholysis. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Observation, since the disease usually remits in five to six years","Oral doxycycline as monotherapy","Rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone, given urgently","Ultrapotent topical steroids alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pemphigus is life-threatening, so treatment is urgent, and a steroid-sparing agent is usually added so steroids can be tapered. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has well-demarcated salmon-pink plaques with thick silvery scale over both elbows, knees and the sacrum. Scraping the scale produces pinpoint bleeding. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nummular eczema","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Lichen simplex chronicus","Plaque psoriasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The extensor distribution with silvery scale and the Auspitz sign (bleeding on scale removal) are classic. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man with two patches of alopecia areata on the scalp asks about treatment. Which is the most appropriate first-line option?","choices":["Oral finasteride","Intralesional corticosteroids","Topical corticosteroids","Topical minoxidil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — first line in adolescents and adults; topical steroids are first line for children aged ten and under. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"An 8-month-old has erythema, scaling and small erosions over the convex surfaces of the buttocks and upper thighs, with the inguinal folds clearly spared. There is no purulence. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Frequent diaper changes, gentle cleansing, air exposure and a thick zinc oxide or petrolatum barrier at each change","A topical antifungal at every change","Oral antibiotics","A high-potency topical corticosteroid applied at every change until the rash has completely cleared"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reducing moisture and irritant exposure is the foundation, and the fold sparing indicates irritant rather than candidal disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with known bilateral stasis dermatitis develops acute swelling and pain in the right calf over two days. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin a topical corticosteroid to the affected calf","Reassure her that unilateral flares are expected in stasis dermatitis","Order venous duplex ultrasonography to evaluate for deep venous thrombosis","Increase the strength of her compression stockings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute unilateral swelling or pain should raise concern for deep venous thrombosis in this setting. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 5-year-old with atopic dermatitis has an area of weeping, thickly crusted plaques with pustules over a previously flaring elbow. She is afebrile. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic test?","choices":["Patch testing","Potassium hydroxide preparation","Bacterial culture of the crusted lesions","Herpes simplex virus polymerase chain reaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — culture is indicated for purulent, pustular or significantly crusted lesions to rule out infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"}]};
